    "evidence_text": "Predicting the Industry of Users on Social Media\tAutomatic profiling of social media users is an important task for supporting a multitude of downstream applications.\n\nWhile a number of studies have used social media content to extract and study collective social attributes, there is a lack of substantial research that addresses the detection of a user's industry.\n\nTo uncloak this information, a number of studies have utilized social media users' footprints to approximate their profiles.\n\nRelated Work\tAlongside the wide adoption of social media by the public, researchers have been leveraging the newly available data to create and refine models of users' behavior and profiling.\n\nThere exists a myriad research that analyzes language in order to profile social media users.\n\nAt the same time, a number of researchers sought to predict the social media users' age and/or gender  ,  ,  , while others targeted and analyzed the ethnicity, nationality, and race of the users  ,  ,  .\n\nBoth of these recent studies are based on micro-blogging platforms, which inherently restrict the number of characters that a post can have, and consequently the way that users can express themselves.\n\nBlogger platform provides a total of 39 different industry options.\n\nConclusion\t In this paper, we examined the task of predicting a social media user's industry.",
